---

**Detailed Verification Log**

---

### 1.  Definition of the point set  

> “Let  
> \[
> S_n=\{(a,b)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid a+b\le n+1\},\qquad |S_n|=\frac{n(n+1)}2 .
> \]”

*Correct.*  The set \(S_n\) is exactly the collection of required points; the cardinality formula follows from the well‑known triangular number count.

---

### 2.  Reduction to vertical non‑sunny lines  

> “Replace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line.  A vertical line covers **all** points of a column, whereas a horizontal line or a line of slope \(-1\) can meet a column in at most one point.  Hence after the replacement we still cover every point of \(S_n\) and we have exactly the same number \(k\) of sunny lines.  
> Consequently we may (and shall) assume that the \(n-k\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical lines  
> \[
> x=1,\;x=2,\dots ,x=n-k .
> \tag{1.1}
> \]”

*Issue (Justification Gap).*  
The argument that the replacement preserves coverage is only sketched.  One must show that for each non‑sunny line \(L\) there exists a column not yet occupied by a vertical line such that after replacing \(L\) by the vertical line \(x=c\) all points formerly covered by \(L\) remain covered (either by the new vertical line or by other lines already present).  The solution asserts this without a rigorous case analysis (e.g. handling the situation where all columns intersected by \(L\) are already occupied).  Consequently the reduction is plausible but not proved.

---

### 3.  Description of the remaining points  

> “All points with abscissa \(a\le n-k\) are already covered.  The remaining points are  
> \[
> T_k=\{(x,y)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid x+y\le k+1\},
> \tag{1.2}
> \]”

*Correct.*  After covering columns \(1,\dots ,n-k\) the uncovered points are precisely those with \(x\in\{n-k+1,\dots ,n\}\) and \(x+y\le n+1\); translating \(x\mapsto x-(n-k)\) yields the set \(T_k\).  The reduction is therefore valid provided the previous step is justified.

---

### 4.  Integer form of a sunny line  

> “Multiplying its equation by a common denominator we obtain an integer equation  
> \[
> p x-q y=c ,\qquad \gcd(p,q)=1,\;q>0,\;p\neq0,\;p\neq -q .
> \tag{2.1}
> \]”

*Correct.*  Any non‑vertical line can be written in this form; the conditions exclude horizontal (\(p=0\)) and slope \(-1\) (\(p=-q\)) lines, exactly the non‑sunny cases.

---

### 5.  Parametrisation of integer points on the line  

> “All integer points on the line are described by (3) above:  
> \[
> (x,y)=(x_0+qt,\;y_0+pt),\qquad t\in\mathbb Z .
> \tag{2.2}
> \]”

*Correct.*  Standard description of the lattice points on a line with coprime direction vector \((q,p)\).

---

### 6.  Behaviour of the sum \(x+y\)  

> “Their sums satisfy (4):  
> \[
> x+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .
> \tag{2.3}
> \]”

*Correct.*  Immediate from (2.2).

---

### 7.  Bounding the number of points of a sunny line inside \(T_k\)  

> “Because the line is sunny, \(p+q\neq0\); therefore the sum changes by at least \(|p+q|\ge1\) when \(t\) varies by one.  
> Inside the triangle \(T_k\) we have \(2\le x+y\le k+1\).  From (2.3) the number of admissible integers \(t\) is at most  
> \[
> \Bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1-2}{|p+q|}\Bigr\rfloor+1\le
> \Bigl\lfloor\frac{k-1}{2}\Bigr\rfloor+1=
> \Bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1}{2}\Bigr\rfloor .
> \tag{2.4}
> \]”

*Correct for \(|p+q|\ge2\).*  The inequality follows because \(|p+q|\ge2\) implies \(\frac{k-1}{|p+q|}\le\frac{k-1}{2}\).

> “If \(|p+q|=1\) then \(p+q=\pm1\).  The equality \(p+q=1\) forces \(q\ge2\) (otherwise \(p=0\) which would give a horizontal line).  Because \(x=x_0+qt\) must stay in the interval \([1,k]\), the number of admissible \(t\) is at most \(\bigl\lfloor k/q\bigr\rfloor+1\le\bigl\lfloor(k+1)/2\bigr\rfloor\).  
