*Issue (Justification Gap).*  
The claim “\(q\ge2\)” is **false** for the case \(p+q=-1\): taking \(p=-2,\;q=1\) gives a sunny line of slope \(-2\) with \(q=1\).  The bound \(\bigl\lfloor k/q\bigr\rfloor+1\le\bigl\lfloor(k+1)/2\bigr\rfloor\) still holds for \(q=1\) (because \(\lfloor k/1\rfloor+1 = k+1\) and for odd \(k\) we have \(k+1 = \frac{k+1}{2}\cdot2\ge\frac{k+1}{2}\)).  Nevertheless the argument as written is incomplete; a correct justification must treat the \(q=1\) case separately.

> “Hence (2.4) holds for **every** sunny line, and the bound is sharp (see §4).”

*Conclusion.*  The bound \(|\text{sunny line}\cap T_k|\le\lfloor\frac{k+1}{2}\rfloor\) is true, but the proof contains a gap concerning the case \(|p+q|=1\) with \(q=1\).

---

### 8.  Counting argument  

> “If we have exactly \(k\) sunny lines, (2.5) yields  
> \[
> k\Bigl\lfloor\frac{k+1}{2}\Bigr\rfloor\ge\frac{k(k+1)}2 .
> \tag{3.2}
> \]”

*Correct.*  Multiplying the per‑line bound by the number of sunny lines gives a lower bound for the total number of points covered; comparing with \(|T_k|=k(k+1)/2\) yields (3.2).

> “*Even \(k\).* … Hence **no even \(k\ge2\) is possible**.  
