> “For \(k=2m+1\) the corresponding lines are exactly the three lines listed in (10).  A direct substitution shows that each of them contains \(m+1\) points of \(T_{2m+1}\) and that the three point‑sets are disjoint.  No other sunny line can contain \(m+1\) points, because any other choice would have \(|p+q|\ge3\) or \(|p+q|=1\) with \(q\ge2\), and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound.”

*Issue (Justification Gap).*  
The claim that “no other sunny line can contain \(m+1\) points” is justified only by a brief reference to (2.4).  A fully rigorous argument would need to examine all possibilities for \((p,q)\) with \(\gcd(p,q)=1\) and show that the bound in (2.4) is strict unless \((p,q)\) is one of the three listed pairs.  The solution sketches this but does not provide the exhaustive case analysis.

